desc:
This course covers various issues relevant to bilingualism and bilingual education. Discussion includes theoretical aspects (e.g., definitions of bilingualism, languages in society, development of bilingualism, bilingualism and cognition) and pedagogical issues (e.g., types of bilingual education, education for bilingualism, effectiveness of bilingual education, effective schools and classrooms for bilingual students). Discussions will center on issues on bilingualism and bilingual education that will have implications for English language teaching in Japan, including immersion education and foreign language in elementary school (FLES).

Objectives:
1. To provide students with an overview of the conceptual bases of bilingualism and bilingual education;

2. To discuss bilingualism in terms of both psychological (e.g., identity) and social aspects;

3. To discuss the effectiveness of bilingual education, which will help us implement an innovative English language program in Japan;

4. To familiarize students with bilingual education mainly in Japan and the United States.

Anthropological research process involves a series of activities to delve into the essence of culture and gain a comprehensive understanding of others. This course introduces diverse anthropological methods with Chinese ethnographic cases, and aims to lead students to be more prepared for stepping into therealm of reality and complete their fieldwork——the soul of modern anthropological methodology. The intricate nature of China and the diversity of anthropological methods complement each other in this course. With its ancient civilization and complex tapestry of diverse ethnic groups and cultures,various research methods can unveil multiple facets of China, while numerous ethnographic cases surrounding China demonstrate the efficacy of different anthropological approaches. Through exploration, students are expected toacquire fundamental skills in fieldwork and develop a nuanced understanding of Chinese culture from an anthropological perspective.After learning various methods, each student who selectsd the course can independently complete a small anthropological research.

Objectives:
To introduce and lead students to master a variety of anthropological fieldwork methods;

To present a multi-dimensional China by discussing various ethnographic cases;

To motivate students'curiosities and encourage them to come up with new research projects, meanwhile putting these methods into practice.

Given the complexity of modernity, how should we understand China in the 21st century? Whether you are a native Chinese speaker or not, "Understanding China" has now become an independent study field and an topic of daily life. Through anthropological studies, the course will explore key themes such as village community and economic life/family and kinship/gender, emotion, and private life/religion and folk belief. Ethnographic works employing a range of anthropological methods will be used to illustrate how different social worlds within China are produced and experienced. It also attends to embodied and emotional dimensions of social life by examining everyday practices related to food, Chinese medicine, and rituals surrounding life and death. By grounding broader social questions in concrete practices, the course connects large-scale transformations with ordinary experiences.

Bringing together classic and contemporary anthropological studies, this course invites cross-cultural discussion and comparison, encouraging students to develop a nuanced and critical understanding of China as a complex, internally diverse, and continuously transforming society.

Objectives:
To enhance students‘ fundamental understanding of 20th-century China from a kaleidoscope of perspectives and to plant the seeds of curiosity and nurture potential research interests and goals about the complicated realm of China;

To lead students to understand the different paradigms, theories, and methods of anthropological research;

To lead students to possess essential skills of critical thinking, reading, and presenting social science works, as well as the ability to work in groups.

Labor is not only a classic theme in social science, but also a vital lens for understanding social development over the past century. This course introduces an anthropological way of understanding labor by focusing on how different forms of labor are made possible, organized, and evaluated under specific material and technological conditions. Rather than treating labor as an abstract or universal activity, the course examines labor as situated practice: shaped by tools, infrastructures, spatial arrangements, temporal regimes, and institutional frameworks.Through theoretical readings and ethnographic case studies, students will learn to analyze how concrete conditions give rise to particular forms of social relations, power asymmetries, moral expectations, and modes of subjectivity.

This course will tell the stories of different laborers,aiming not only to lead students to master multiple situations of labor (especially in the Chinese context), but also encourage a reflexive exploration of students finding their own positions in society and life.

Objectives:
To strengthen the understanding of the ‘labor’ concept and living conditions of laborers in the Chinese context and the transition of Chinese society;

To learn how to regard ‘labor’ from the perspective of anthropological holistic approaches;

To lead students to reassess their career planning and cultivate a better understanding of others.

Filmis a kind of complex text with multiple layers. We can enter the interior of a film in many ways: story motif, narrative structure, photographic composition, lens aesthetics, social implications, cultural criticism,etc. Different from other film study courses, this course has several outstanding features:

1.This course aims to lead students to an appreciation of the unique aesthetics of each film from a holistic perspective. The way of appreciating comes from the above-mentioned blending of multiple ways of understanding films, and the chosen films often achieve a highly unity in their form and content;

2. In this course, visual works for appreciation include both fictional and non-fictional pieces. We will explore works from author-directors,documentaries, and anthropological films (also known as visual ethnography).They will be categorized into different themes and we will see different expressions of the same theme in different films with a comparative discussion with films from other cultures;This comparison will show anthropological implicationsin a sense.

3. The selected films are all Chinese language films. This means that we will enter the context of Chinese culture through the film text. It is not only the lines told in Chinese and the scenes taken on the ground in China. It also means “the Chinese emotional presentation” and "the Chinese way of viewing”. We will discuss a range of possibilities arising from the collision of film as an art form with the Chinese context.

Objectives:
Strengthen the aesthetic ability of visual works；

Cultivate an appreciation of films from the perspective of anthropology;

Comprehend Chinese language and context better by appreciating Chinese visual works;

Be potential for creating own work.

The teaching methodology of this class is F2F style.The principal aim of this course is to familiarize students with the methodology of social sciences. The course will basically cover the nature and distinguishing features of the scientific knowledge and survey the basic processes of research methodology as practices in the social sciences. The general goal of this course is to introduce and develop skills needed to conceptualize a problem, make use of available literature, design a research strategy, evaluate, organize and derive useful solutions based on knowledge. This course asks students to read closely great texts with a focus on Occidental and Japanese traditions developed from classical sources. The course also asks students to construct arguments of their own, both in speech and in writing, about some of the explicit and implicit issues these texts are raising.

Objectives:
In addition, as a result of having completed the requirements of this course, the students are expected to:

(1) Understand the scientific process, its application in the studies;

(2) Appreciate the value of critical thinking;

(3) Think and argue critically and logically about issues;

(4) Have an ability to express each point of view in public;

(5) Acquire the research skills necessary to the assessment and interpretation of existing research.

This course will be given in Japanese and introduce students to contemporary logic of proposition, from a philosophical point of view. I will compare propositional logic with other representative trends in the field, such as formal, transcendental, dialectical, and phenomenological logic.

The students will learn to solve basic problems such as: What is a proposition? Where is the difference between logic and language? Is it possible to define truth? Why must we avoid contradictions?

Objectives:
The objectives of this course are as follows:

1) to develop a simple logical picture of the world;

2) to learn the role and aim of logic;

3) to understand logic from a philosophical point of view;

4) to improve student’s critical and logical skills.

In this course, we will examine the topic of postmodernism.
Postmodernism is a philosophical and cultural movement that emerged in the late twentieth century and exerted a profound influence across a wide range of fields. Some critics argue that its impact extended even to economics and politics; however, this course will focus primarily on art, culture, literature, and philosophy.

Examples discussed in class will include the following materials:

1. The Beatles’ Sgt. Pepper’s Lonely Hearts Club Band and other musical works;

2. John Barth’s Lost in the Funhouse and other postmodernist short stories in American literature;

3. Jacques Derrida’s philosophical concept of deconstruction;

4. Yoko Ono’s experimental artworks;

5. Graphic novels such as Art Spiegelman’s Maus and G. B. Tran’s Vietnamerica;

6. American films such as The Truman Show and Edward Scissorhands;

7. Dinh Q. Lê’s installation art.

Objectives:
To understand the idea of postmodernism and see examples of artistic and cultural representations of that idea.

