Digital humanities is a field that aims to produce new information by looking at humanities research through a computational lens, by preserving human culture through digital media and digitization, and studying how the digital affects humanity. Digital humanities is an interdisciplinary field at the intersection of humanities, arts, and social sciences on the one hand, and computing on the other. It allows scholars of more traditional fields to explore pertinent data from a new perspective and is especially useful where qualitative approaches, such as close reading, are not possible due to the amount of data.
In this course, the students will be introduced to the main concepts of digital humanities and gain an understanding of why and how digital humanities is practiced. The students will learn about current topics in digital humanities research and learn about simple tools, resources, and methods for digital humanities. Students will also learn critical thinking and method literacy in relation to computational methodologies. This course is a great stepping stone into more advanced courses on related topics. The final project topic depends on student interests and background and can be either a theoretical overview or a report of a digital humanities-related programming task.

Objectives:
After successful completion of this course, the students will:

・Understand what digital humanities is and what kind of research is done within digital humanities;

・Know what kinds of tools and resources exist, for what, when, and how they are used;

・Know how to access online data sources such as corpora, historical databases, and data sets;

・Be able to interpret digital humanities research and evaluate suitability of methods for different tasks

The teaching methodology of this class is F2F style.
The aim of this course consists of the fact to detail the actors in international relations, how foreign policy is made, and the role of power.
The course examines contemporary and future problems in the international system, including military conflict, economics, demography, and the environment.
Students will receive an advanced knowledge of the discipline of international relations through an overview of international security, foreign policy, international organizations, international law, and international political economy.
Students examine the basic structures, processes, and issues relating to international affairs.

Objectives:
Upon completion of this course, students should have a strong basic understanding of international relations and will be able to:

a) Identify the roles and influence of state and non-state actors;

b) Define the levels of analysis in IR;

c) Understand how governments make foreign policy and decision-making processes;

d) Understand the causes of war, including conflicts of interest, conflicts of ideas;

e) Understand the means of waging war;

f) Define the nature of military force and describe the role military force plays in politics;

g) Describe the functions and organization of the UN;

h) Describe the evolution and sources of international law.

Enrich the knowledge about International Relations, including Development and Governance issues in Africa, European Studies, International Security and so forth.

Bring up a broad-minded person.

Thiscourse is designed to improve students’ability to write in English in ways that can help them communicate theirknowledge, gained through experience or research, to non-academic audiences inorder to inform or persuade them. In order to do this, students will work onidentifying their audience, their purpose in communicating with that audience,and what linguistic means will most effectively reach that audience. We willexamine successful examples of various genres (process, descriptive, andpersuasive) with the goal of understanding how these texts work and howstudents can make use of what they have learned to produce their own texts. Thiscourse uses a workshop model in which students share work and provide eachother with guided constructive feedback to help with revising their essays. Theinstructor will also provide individual consultations before final revisions ofan essay. At the end of the course, students will choose one of their essays tomake a presentation about.

Objectives:
After taking this course, students will be able to:

1. Recognize the features of various written genres and identify the audience that texts are aimed at;

2. Critique essays in terms of the effectiveness of their writing for achieving their purpose;

3. Write effective process, descriptive, and persuasive essays, using English to connect their purpose to their audience;

4. Provide constructive feedback on essays to other writers to help them improve their works.
